# Raw BPIC 2014 data is not redistributed here.  Fetch it from the DOI in
# README.md and place it in data/raw/.
#
# `data/*` rather than `data/`: git does not descend into an ignored
# DIRECTORY, so a blanket `data/` makes every later negation dead.  Round
# eighteen ignored `data/` and the manuscript said the adjudication extracts
# were committed; `git ls-files data/` returned nothing.
data/*

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Excel lock files
~$*.xlsx

# LaTeX build products.  build/ also holds aaai2027.sty and aaai2027.bst,
# which are AAAI's and are fetched by scripts/build_paper.py, not vendored.
build/
*.aux
*.bbl
*.blg
*.log
*.out
*.pdf
*.synctex.gz
!paper/iaai27_empty_cmdb.pdf
#  The two documents actually submitted.  `*.pdf' above ignores build
#  products, and these are build products -- but they are also the
#  deliverable, and a reader who clones this repository should get the
#  article and its supplement without having to run LaTeX first.
!paper/specification_surfaces.pdf
!paper/supplement.pdf

# Superseded workbooks from a withdrawn analysis; kept on disk, out of the repo.
capability_readiness_matrix.xlsx
capability_readiness_matrix_filled.xlsx

# Run logs from scripts/reproduce_all.py.  Regenerated on every run;
# the numbers they contain live in results/*.csv, which IS tracked.
logs/

# The corpus is fetched by DOI, never redistributed.  CHECKSUMS.txt is tracked.
!data/corpus/
data/corpus/*
!data/corpus/CHECKSUMS.txt

# The corruption suite writes this while it runs.  A commit made during a
# suite run captured a CORRUPTED manuscript in round seventeen; see HANDOFF
# section 20.11.  It is ignored so that cannot happen again.
paper/.tex.bak

# Audit full texts: other people's PDFs, extracted.  Not redistributed.
# scripts/r40_audit.py --fetch regenerates them from the DOIs in
# results/r40_sample.csv.  The adjudication extracts ARE committed, because
# they are the evidence the human codes were assigned from.
data/audit/fulltext/
data/audit/meta/

# Round nineteen's pilot.  Same rule: the full texts and the index metadata
# are other people's and are re-fetched by DOI; the DOSSIERS and the
# ADJUDICATIONS are committed, because they are the evidence every judgement
# was made from and the manuscript points a reader at them.
# The DOSSIERS are not committed either: they are extracts of other people's
# copyrighted full texts, and a systematic-review evidence table that
# redistributes twenty sentences from each of 174 papers is redistribution.
# They are REGENERATED by `python scripts/s06_audit2.py --dossiers` from the
# re-fetched full texts, deterministically, so a reader can rebuild exactly
# the packets each judgement was made from.  What IS committed is the
# judgement itself, with its free-text reason, which is the author's own work.
!data/audit2/
data/audit2/*
!data/audit2/adjudication_in.csv
!data/audit2/adjudication_out.csv

# The worked example's cached download.  Fetched by DOI on first run.
data/example/
.test_count.json
